---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

// Change this if a different address should receive privacy mail.
const contact = 'privacy@bikes.pizza';
// Requests to take a published post down go here rather than to privacy mail.
const removals = 'remove.post@bikes.pizza';
// Everything that is neither a privacy request nor a takedown.
const general = 'contact@bikes.pizza';
const updated = 'September 9, 2026';
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy Policy · bikes.pizza"
  description="What bikes.pizza collects, why, and how to have it deleted."
>
  <div class="container mx-auto px-6 py-16 max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-light tracking-wide text-ink">Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted text-sm font-light">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <div class="mt-12 space-y-10 text-ink font-light leading-relaxed">
      <section>
        <p>
          bikes.pizza is a place to share photographs of bicycles and pizza. This
          policy covers the bikes.pizza website and the bikes.pizza apps for iOS
          and Android, all operated by Pizza Predator LLC.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          The short version: we collect what an account and a photo submission
          need, and nothing else. There is no analytics SDK, no advertising, no
          third-party tracker, and no profile built about you. We do not sell
          personal information, and we never have. Photos of people are refused
          automatically, and nothing is published without a human reviewer's
          approval.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">What we collect</h2>

        <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg text-ink">Your account</h3>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Signing up stores your email address and an account identifier. You can
          sign in with an email address and password, with Google, or with Apple.
          When you sign in with Google or Apple we receive your email address from
          them; if you use Apple's Hide My Email, we only ever see the relay
          address Apple gives us, not your real one.
        </p>

        <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg text-ink">Passkeys and two-factor codes</h3>
        <p class="mt-2">
          If you create a passkey we store its public key, its credential
          identifier, and when it was created and last used. The private key stays
          on your device or in your password manager and is never sent to us — it
          is not something we can read, copy, or hand over. If you enroll an
          authenticator app instead, we store the shared secret that verifies your
          codes.
        </p>

        <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg text-ink">Submissions</h3>
        <p class="mt-2">
          When you submit a photo we store the image together with the title,
          description, and name you attach to it, the account it came from
          (including the email address on that account), and the time it arrived.
          Your email address is never published.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          Please read the rest of this part carefully, because it is the one place
          where this policy asks something of you. A submission is an offer of the
          photo to the public. By submitting it, you grant Pizza Predator LLC a
          non-exclusive, worldwide, royalty-free, perpetual license to store,
          review, reproduce, resize, publish, and display the photo and its
          accompanying text on bikes.pizza and in the bikes.pizza apps, as part of
          a lasting public archive of bicycles and pizza. If a reviewer approves
          the submission it becomes a post, published with the title, description,
          and name you supplied, and posts are intended to remain available
          indefinitely as a historical record.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          Posts are not part of your account. A post you submitted is retained, and
          remains public, after your account is deleted, whoever deletes it and for
          whatever reason. The section "Published posts and requests for removal"
          below explains what can and cannot be done about that.
        </p>

        <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg text-ink">On your device</h3>
        <p class="mt-2">
          The app and the website keep a little information on your own device —
          whether you are signed in, your light or dark theme choice, and what is
          in your shopping cart. This stays on the device and is not a tracking
          cookie.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">What we do not collect</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          We do not collect your location, your contacts, your health or fitness
          data, your browsing on other sites, or an advertising identifier. The app
          asks for camera and photo library access only so that you can choose a
          picture to submit; it does not read your photo library otherwise, and
          nothing is uploaded until you submit it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">No people, and a review before anything is published</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          bikes.pizza is a site about bicycles and pizza, not about people, and it
          is built to keep people out of the pictures. Nothing you submit is
          published as-is. Every photo passes two checks, in this order, before it
          can appear anywhere on the site or in the apps.
        </p>
        <ol class="mt-4 space-y-3 list-decimal list-outside pl-6">
          <li>
            <span class="text-ink">An automatic check, at the moment of
            submission.</span> Before a photo is stored, it is sent to Google
            Cloud Vision, which reports whether it appears to contain a face or a
            person-shaped figure, and whether it appears to contain adult, violent,
            or racy content. A photo that reaches our threshold on any of those is
            refused on the spot: it is not saved, not queued, and not seen by a
            reviewer, and you are asked to choose a different photo. This is how we
            keep imagery of people off the site proactively rather than after the
            fact. The check is a filter applied to the image; it is not used to
            identify anyone, and the result is kept only as a note on the
            submission for the reviewer.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="text-ink">A human review, before publication.</span> A
            photo that passes the automatic check goes to a reviewer, who decides
            whether to publish it, queue it, or reject it. Nothing becomes a post
            without that approval.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="mt-4">
          A word about the limits of this. Automated detection is not perfect, and
          a photo with no person in it can still contain something the submitter
          did not notice or did not intend to share: a reflection, a document on a
          table, a license plate, a name on a mailbox, an address, something in the
          background that is private, embarrassing, or unfortunate. The reviewer
          makes an honest, good-faith effort to catch such things and not to
          publish them, and rejects a photo when in doubt. That effort is a
          commitment to care, not a guarantee: we cannot promise that nothing of
          the kind will ever be published. Before you submit a photo, look at all
          of it, not just the bike or the pizza, because once it is published it
          is subject to the retention and removal terms in "Published posts and
          requests for removal", and the fact that something slipped past review
          is one of the reasons we give weight to when a removal is requested.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">Who else processes your data</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          We use a small number of service providers, each handling only what its
          job requires:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 list-disc list-outside pl-6">
          <li><span class="text-ink">Google Firebase</span> — accounts and sign-in, the database, file storage for photos, and the server functions behind the app.</li>
          <li><span class="text-ink">Google Cloud Vision</span> — the automated photo check described above.</li>
          <li><span class="text-ink">Sanity</span> — stores the posts that have been published to the site.</li>
          <li><span class="text-ink">Mailgun</span> — sends the notification email that tells a reviewer a submission is waiting.</li>
          <li><span class="text-ink">Shopify</span> — runs the shop. If you buy something, your order and payment details are handled by Shopify under their privacy policy; we never see your card number.</li>
          <li><span class="text-ink">Cloudflare</span> — serves the website and its domain.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">Deleting your account</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          You can delete your account yourself, at any time, from the Settings
          screen in the app or from the account page on this website. Deleting an
          account removes your sign-in details, your email address as an account
          identifier, your passkeys, and any authenticator enrollment.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          Deleting your account does not delete posts that have already been
          published, and it does not delete the record of the submissions behind
          them. Those are kept as described in the next section.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">Published posts and requests for removal</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Because posts are published as part of a public archive, they are treated
          differently from account information. The following applies to every
          post, regardless of who submitted it or whether that person still has an
          account.
        </p>
        <ol class="mt-4 space-y-3 list-decimal list-outside pl-6">
          <li>
            <span class="text-ink">Retention.</span> Published posts, and the
            images in them, are retained indefinitely. We do not remove a post
            automatically when the account that submitted it is deleted, when any
            period of time has passed, or on any other automatic trigger; a post or
            an image leaves the site only through a request under this section.
            The submission record behind a post, including the account and email
            address it was submitted from, is kept with the post so that its
            origin is known.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="text-ink">Requests.</span> Anyone may ask for a post to be
            removed by writing to{' '}
            <a href={`mailto:${removals}`} class="text-spice hover:underline">{removals}</a>{' '}
            with a link to the post and the reason for the request. Each request is
            considered individually.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="text-ink">No guarantee.</span> Removal is at our
            discretion. We give weight to reasons such as: the photo shows you,
            your home, or your property and you did not intend it to be public; it
            was submitted by someone who had no right to submit it; it contains
            something that should not have passed review; or the law requires its
            removal. We are under no obligation to grant a request, and we may
            decline one, in particular where the post has become part of the record
            this site exists to keep.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="text-ink">Timing.</span> A request that is granted is acted
            on within a reasonable time. We do not undertake to act immediately.
            After a post is removed from bikes.pizza, copies may continue to exist
            for a period in caches, in content delivery networks, in search engine
            indexes, in backups, and with anyone who copied the post while it was
            public. Those copies are outside our control, and this policy makes no
            promise about their removal.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="text-ink">What removal means.</span> Removing a post takes
            it off the public site and out of the apps. The image itself, and the
            submission record behind it, are kept: by default they are retained
            after removal, so that the removal and the reason for it are
            documented. If you want the image deleted as well, say so in your
            request. Deletion of the image may be granted, on the same terms and
            with the same discretion as removal of the post, and is likewise not
            immediate.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="text-ink">Legal rights.</span> If the law where you live
            gives you a right to have personal information erased, that right
            applies to a published post only to the extent the law provides, and it
            is subject to the exceptions the law allows, including exceptions for
            archiving, for freedom of expression, and for establishing or defending
            legal claims. A request made under such a law goes to the same address
            and is answered as the law requires.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">Keeping it</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Account information is kept for as long as the account exists. Rejected
          submissions are kept only long enough to handle any question about the
          decision. Published posts, the images in them, and the submission
          records behind them are kept indefinitely; a post or an image may be
          removed on request, as described under "Published posts and requests for
          removal".
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">Children</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          bikes.pizza is not directed at children under 13, and we do not knowingly
          collect information from them. If you believe a child has created an
          account, write to us and we will remove it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">Your rights</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          You can ask us for a copy of the information we hold about you, ask us to
          correct it, or ask us to delete it. Depending on where you live you may
          have further rights over that information, including under the GDPR or
          the CCPA. Write to the address below and we will help.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          These rights concern your personal information: your account details and
          the records tied to them. They do not by themselves entitle you to the
          removal of a published post. A post is public content that you offered
          for publication, and a request to remove it is handled as described under
          "Published posts and requests for removal", taking account of any legal
          right you have.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">Changes</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          If this policy changes we will update the date at the top of this page.
          Substantial changes will be announced on the site.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light text-ink">Contact</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Questions about privacy, or a request about your data, can go to{' '}
          <a href={`mailto:${contact}`} class="text-spice hover:underline">{contact}</a>.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          To ask for a published post to be removed, write to{' '}
          <a href={`mailto:${removals}`} class="text-spice hover:underline">{removals}</a>{' '}
          instead, with a link to the post and your reason. Requests are considered
          individually and are not guaranteed to be granted.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          General questions and comments about bikes.pizza are welcome at{' '}
          <a href={`mailto:${general}`} class="text-spice hover:underline">{general}</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
